 runs, total time determined placement."]] [:tr {} [:th {:valign "top"} "Details"] [:td {} "Gates: 65" [:br {}] "Length: ?" [:br {}] "Start Altitude: 1890 m" [:br {}] "Vertical Drop: 220 m"]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The men’s slalom was definitely affected by the weather as the two runs were conducted during a snowstorm, and midway thru the first run, a mild earthquake (Richter scale of 5.0) struck Mt. Yakebitai. The event came down to a virtual team battle between the Austrians and Norwegians. Austrian " [:a {:href "/athletes/98047"} "Thomas Sykora"] " had led the 1997 World Cup and was leading in the 1997-98 season as well, and came from an athletic family, his mother, " [:a {:href "/athletes/64862"} "Liesel Prokop"] ", having won an Olympic silver medal in athletics in the 1968 pentathlon. Sykora led on the first run, followed by Norwegians " [:a {:href "/athletes/99508"} "Hans Petter Buraas"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/85887"} "Ole Kristian Furuseth"] ". In fourth was " [:a {:href "/athletes/97765"} "Thomas Stangassinger"] " of Austria, the defending champion, followed by two more sons of Norway, " [:a {:href "/athletes/97849"} "Tom Stiansen"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/87433"} "Finn Christian Jagge"] ". The second run was dominated by Buraas who moved up to win the gold medal, his run 0.69 seconds ahead of another Austrian " [:a {:href "/athletes/95269"} "Christian Mayer"] ", who had only been equal eighth on the first run, and could move up only to fifth place overall. Sykora and Stangassinger both skied poorly on the second run, finishing in a tie for 12th, and would drop down. Sykora hung on to win a bronze medal, while Stangassinger dropped to sixth. Furuseth placed fourth on the second run to win the silver medal. In all, the first seven places were taken by Austrians and Norwegians, the first finisher from another country was " [:a {:href "/athletes/99513"} "Joël Chenal"] " of France, in eighth."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-st